    "title": "The Infrastructure of Agent-Scale Coding: What Actually Changed",
    "question": "What concrete changes have AI coding agents forced in the tools, platforms, and workflows that developers rely on, and why do those changes matter now?",
    "argument": "AI coding agents are not just faster autocomplete—they are generating enough code volume and concurrent activity to break the infrastructure built for human-paced development. The measurable responses: platforms rebuilding Git storage, databases redesigning control planes, and deployment tools adding guardrailed agent workflows. These are supply-side changes that precede developer adoption decisions, and understanding them helps student developers evaluate which platform promises rest on real architectural work versus marketing.",
    "synthesis": "Three independent sources converge on a single structural shift: agent-generated code volume is now large enough to require infrastructure redesign rather than incremental scaling. GitHub reports Git events growing from 218.2B to 473.3B per month in one year and commits growing 5× year-over-year, and attributes this directly to agent workloads requiring an architectural separation of storage from compute (source-5). Cloudflare reports 15× growth in D1 databases over one year and explicitly names 'AI-assisted development and vibe coding platforms' as the driver, prompting a control-plane redesign that routes queries without touching centralized Postgres (source-2). Anthropic, via Simon Willison's commentary, reports daily code merges rising from ~2,500 to ~30,000 over six months, forcing a replacement of Git itself with a custom system called Scion (source-13). These are three independent platforms each reporting infrastructure-breaking growth from agent workloads. An alternative explanation—that organic human developer growth accounts for these numbers—is contradicted by the 5× commit growth rate in one year and Anthropic's explicit attribution. The Void SDK/CLI (source-1) and the Copilot customization hierarchy (source-8) represent the tooling layer responding to the same shift: if agents are provisioning databases and running deployments autonomously, teams need deployment tools and context files that encode conventions without manual re-explanation. GitHub's secret scanning classifier replacement (source-4) and the secret exposure data (source-7) show the security consequence: screened pushes grew 2.84× while the mean revocation time for leaked secrets remains ~40 days, because human remediation cannot scale with agent-generated code volume. The Stack Overflow maturity model (source-17) and LLM operability guide (source-16) supply the operational framework: a six-level progression from 'agents propose only' to full production-grade operability, with specific checkpoints (eval gate, kill switch, audit ledger) that make the infrastructure changes actionable for student teams. Missing voices: developers on free-tier plans who cannot afford Cloudflare Workers for Platforms, PostgreSQL, or Redis-backed rate limiters; teams outside the GitHub/Cloudflare ecosystem; developers who experienced data loss or unexpected billing from agent-provisioned resources.",
